
<p>Emma Roth, The Verge (gift link):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Google is rebranding its Spark AI agent <a href="https://gemini.google/overview/productivity/">as “Tasks”</a> and making
it free to use. The <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/products/gemini-app/tasks-in-gemini-free-new-features/">feature, which is powered</a> by Google’s new
Gemini 4 Hydrogen AI model, can do things like create meal plans,
manage your calendar, and dig through your emails to find
recurring expenses you might not need.</p>

<p>Users in the US will get access to a few other features, including
the ability for the AI agent to call businesses to compare product
pricing and check inventory, purchase items with Google Pay, and
sign into accounts that support Google Password Manager. US users
can also assign Gemini tasks by emailing gemini@google.com or
texting it.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>New rule for Google: measure twice and cut once when naming anything. Is there a single product they’ve come out with related to AI that hasn’t gone through at least two entirely different names? And do these ding-dongs realize that “Tasks” sounds like a to-do app — in fact, so much like a to-do app that <a href="https://workspace.google.com/products/tasks/">Google already has one named “Google Tasks”</a>?</p>

